WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.047
(Percussion music)

00:00:33.655 --> 00:00:37.659
(Applause)

00:00:37.659 --> 00:00:44.499
Melaku Belay: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:00:45.875 --> 00:00:49.504
Mehret Mandefro: The dance you just saw
is my version of Eskista,

00:00:49.546 --> 00:00:51.881
a traditional dance from Ethiopia.

00:00:52.757 --> 00:00:57.012
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:06.521
MM: Eskista is deeply embedded
in our way of life.

00:01:06.563 --> 00:01:08.607
Even though it's an ancient dance form,

00:01:08.607 --> 00:01:11.610
it can still tell
contemporary stories of today.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.697
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:01:29.294 --> 00:01:33.256
MM: The soundscape of my short dance
transported me to Mercato,

00:01:33.298 --> 00:01:35.634
an open-air market in Addis Ababa,

00:01:35.634 --> 00:01:39.846
where you can find everything
from horse saddles to spices

00:01:39.888 --> 00:01:41.473
to used cell phones.

00:01:41.765 --> 00:01:47.520
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:01:54.277 --> 00:01:59.824
MM: The sound is of workers pounding
recycled metal into useful objects.

00:02:00.325 --> 00:02:04.954
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:02:08.833 --> 00:02:11.878
MM: Today, many shun
the workers in Mercato.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.046
But I don't.

00:02:13.088 --> 00:02:14.881
I love to dance with them.

00:02:14.923 --> 00:02:19.803
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:02:26.476 --> 00:02:27.852
MM: In the 1930s,

00:02:27.852 --> 00:02:31.731
Mercato was a place where Ethiopians
traded with each other

00:02:31.773 --> 00:02:35.151
away from the prying eyes
of Italian occupiers.

00:02:35.485 --> 00:02:38.363
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:02:46.663 --> 00:02:50.250
MM: It was and still is,
a place of Indigenous trading,

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:53.336
not only of goods
but of knowledge and creativity.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:56.965
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:08.768
MM: But our government wants to destroy
Mercato and many other heritage places

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:11.020
in the name of modern infrastructure.

00:03:11.521 --> 00:03:15.275
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:03:34.461 --> 00:03:38.923
MM: Traditional community spaces
hold Indigenous knowledge and memories

00:03:38.965 --> 00:03:42.093
that are crucial for ushering in
cultural dignity,

00:03:42.135 --> 00:03:44.137
facilitating local economy

00:03:44.179 --> 00:03:46.848
and safeguarding society's well-being.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:48.725
When we lose community spaces,

00:03:48.767 --> 00:03:51.227
we lose hope, connection with each other,

00:03:51.269 --> 00:03:55.398
and opportunities to exchange energy
and build peace together.

00:03:55.774 --> 00:04:02.155
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:04:03.281 --> 00:04:04.616
MM: Dance is my protest,

00:04:04.657 --> 00:04:07.994
and Eskista is its contemporary
expression for me.

00:04:08.953 --> 00:04:14.292
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:04:17.545 --> 00:04:20.965
MM: I love my traditional dance
because it is alive in the moment,

00:04:21.007 --> 00:04:22.675
and it leads us to the future.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:27.514
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:04:42.362 --> 00:04:45.490
MM: I honor the many generations
that came before us

00:04:45.532 --> 00:04:47.158
who have given us our dances

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.994
that are always in the process
of becoming anew.

00:04:50.578 --> 00:04:52.121
I dance to tell stories,

00:04:52.163 --> 00:04:56.042
like the endangerment of Mercato
and other Indigenous spaces,

00:04:56.084 --> 00:04:58.211
in the name of development in Ethiopia.

00:04:59.087 --> 00:05:05.426
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:05:18.064 --> 00:05:22.902
MM: Eskista itself originated
in a moment of improvisation.

00:05:22.902 --> 00:05:25.154
It got its name from a folklore

00:05:25.196 --> 00:05:27.782
where a young woman hypnotized a snake

00:05:27.782 --> 00:05:30.827
by mimicking its own movements and sounds.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:34.205
It is beauty born out
of the desire to survive.

00:05:34.747 --> 00:05:37.667
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:06:05.028 --> 00:06:07.196
MM: Eskista saved my life too.

00:06:07.238 --> 00:06:09.490
In 1998, I was homeless,

00:06:09.532 --> 00:06:11.743
living on the streets of Addis Ababa.

00:06:11.784 --> 00:06:16.331
My street life changed when I found work
as a dancer at Fendika Azmari,

00:06:16.331 --> 00:06:18.583
a traditional nightclub in Addis Ababa.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:23.171
I danced every single night
for 12 years with no salary.

00:06:23.504 --> 00:06:26.549
For the first seven years
after the customers left,

00:06:26.591 --> 00:06:29.135
I turned off the light
and slept under the bar.

00:06:29.928 --> 00:06:33.973
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:06:49.781 --> 00:06:51.950
MM: That's where I dreamt
of telling the world

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:55.620
about my Ethiopia of today
through traditional dance.

00:06:55.662 --> 00:06:57.288
And my dreams came true.

00:06:57.747 --> 00:07:01.459
Along with Fendika’s traditional
musicians, dancers and bands,

00:07:01.459 --> 00:07:03.544
I have performed all around the world.

00:07:04.337 --> 00:07:08.675
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:07:22.897 --> 00:07:24.857
MM: With our rich music and dance,

00:07:24.857 --> 00:07:26.609
we tell contemporary stories

00:07:26.651 --> 00:07:29.570
that challenge the single
narrative of Ethiopia,

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:31.572
of hunger and war,

00:07:31.572 --> 00:07:34.826
stories that express our spirit
of freedom and pride

00:07:34.867 --> 00:07:37.078
that Ethiopia was never colonized.

00:07:38.246 --> 00:07:41.290
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:07:44.794 --> 00:07:48.089
MM: Now I'll dance,
improvising to jazz music.

00:07:49.173 --> 00:07:52.510
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:08:13.948 --> 00:08:15.908
MM: Why jazz, you might ask.

00:08:15.950 --> 00:08:19.871
Jazz, because it's an African
music diasporic tradition

00:08:19.912 --> 00:08:24.292
and its feeling of freedom resonates
in Ethiopia like Eskista does.

00:08:24.709 --> 00:08:28.004
In a way, dancing to jazz
feels like going home.

00:08:28.046 --> 00:08:32.091
A deep connection with my people
and the Ethiopian spirit of freedom.

00:08:32.425 --> 00:08:35.011
Jazz is about returning to yourself.

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:40.933
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:08:43.603 --> 00:08:45.354
MM: I invite you to join me

00:08:45.354 --> 00:08:49.567
and witness how ancient Eskista
lives in the present moment.

00:08:49.609 --> 00:08:50.985
MB: (Speaking in Amharic)

00:08:51.027 --> 00:08:52.195
MM: Thank you.

00:08:52.236 --> 00:08:56.657
(Applause and cheers)

00:09:18.304 --> 00:09:21.766
(Jazz music)

00:14:41.919 --> 00:14:44.672
(Music ends)

00:14:44.713 --> 00:14:48.467
(Applause and cheers)

